# =============================================================================
# Terramentor — Environment Configuration
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values you want to use. A `.env`
# beside the app is read on startup (`npm run dev`, `npm run standalone`,
# `npm run desktop`); if there is none, the app runs on its defaults. Anything
# already set in the real environment — which is how the container is
# configured — wins over the file.
# All variables are OPTIONAL — the app works out of the box without any of
# these, but some features will be limited.
#
# Everything listed here is read by the code; nothing here is aspirational.
# Settings stored in the app (Settings → …) win over the environment unless a
# note below says otherwise.
# =============================================================================


# --- Optional: Network binding & port ----------------------------------------
# By default the server binds to 127.0.0.1 (loopback only), so it is NOT directly
# reachable from your LAN or the internet — reach it remotely through an
# authenticated tunnel such as `tailscale serve` (which proxies to localhost).
# Set HOST=0.0.0.0 ONLY if you deliberately want to expose it on all interfaces
# (behind a firewall / on a trusted LAN) — and then set a password in
# Settings → Security so the port isn't an open door to your data.
#
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# PORT=3001


# --- Optional: Who may call the API from a browser ---------------------------
# The API refuses cross-origin browser requests: both supported deployments
# (Vite's /api proxy in dev, the single-origin standalone build) are same-origin,
# so any other page calling this API is a page that has no business here. The
# Host header is validated separately, because DNS rebinding makes an Origin
# check pass by construction.
#
# Loopback, private LAN ranges, *.ts.net (Tailscale), *.local and the server's
# own host are allowed already — you only need these if you are deliberately
# embedding the app somewhere else. Comma-separated.
#
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://study.example.com
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=study.example.com


# --- Optional: Let the app fetch from private addresses ----------------------
# Captured URLs and AI-curated resource links are fetched server-side, and the
# model picks some of those URLs itself — so private, loopback, link-local and
# CGNAT targets are refused (every redirect hop is re-checked). Set this to 1
# only if you want captures to reach something on your own LAN, e.g. a local
# wiki. Your self-hosted SearXNG URL is exempt already and does not need this.
#
# ALLOW_PRIVATE_FETCH=1


# --- Optional: Where the data lives ------------------------------------------
# The SQLite file holds almost all of it. Docker sets DB_PATH to the mounted
# volume; set it yourself for a custom data directory or an isolated test
# instance.
# VAULT_ROOT is the other half: files too big to want inside SQLite, stored
# beside it and content-addressed — vault originals, and the pictures and audio
# from an imported Anki deck. Back up BOTH, or the library keeps rows pointing
# at files it can no longer serve. Docker puts them on the same volume.
#
# DATA_DIR is the third option: one directory holding both (database, vault,
# logs). The desktop launcher sets it to the per-user app-data folder; a
# portable copy sets it to a `data` folder beside the app. DB_PATH / VAULT_ROOT
# win over it when both are given. See server/paths.js and docs/DESKTOP.md.
#
# DATA_DIR=/home/me/.local/share/Terramentor
# DB_PATH=./server/terramentor.db
# VAULT_ROOT=./server/vault


# --- Optional: API key for an OpenAI-compatible provider ---------------------
# Settings → AI Connection lets you point the app at any OpenAI-compatible
# /v1 endpoint (llama-swap, llama.cpp llama-server, LM Studio, OpenRouter,
# OpenAI, ...). The API key can be pasted in Settings (stored in the local
# SQLite DB) — or kept here instead; the Settings value wins if both are set.
#
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# AI_API_KEY=            # alias, same effect


# --- Optional: Override the AI provider without changing Settings ------------
# UNLIKE the API key above, these OVERRIDE the stored Settings row. That is the
# point: a batch tool (e.g. `node tools/quiz-audit.mjs`, which sweeps the whole
# library) must be able to run against a local model without silently
# re-pointing the app the learner is using. Prefer the Settings UI for normal
# use; reach for these for scripts, CI and Docker.
#
# AI_PROVIDER=openai            # 'openai' (any /v1 endpoint) | 'ollama'
# AI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8888/v1
# AI_MODEL=qwen3.6-35b-a3b
# AI_OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
#
# How many AI tasks may run at once. Left alone this follows the provider — one
# slot for Ollama or any loopback endpoint (a local model is usually single-slot,
# so a second request queues rather than adding throughput), three for a hosted
# one. Set it only for an endpoint that does not look like either.
# AI_CONCURRENCY=1
#
# Extra top-level fields (JSON object) merged into every request to an
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint — chat, vision and embeddings. The /v1 protocol is
# a floor, and some endpoints understand fields the app has no word for.
#
# The case this was built for is ROUTING PRIVACY on OpenRouter. Their account
# settings carry the "may a provider train on my data" preference account-wide,
# but data RETENTION is decided per request: without `zdr` the default is no
# retention-based filtering at all. If anyone but you is using this install on
# a hosted endpoint, this is the line that keeps their notes off endpoints that
# keep them:
#
# AI_EXTRA_BODY={"provider":{"zdr":true,"data_collection":"deny"}}
#
# THE SAME FIELD PICKS WHO SERVES THE REQUEST, and on a router that matters
# more than it sounds. A model id on a router is not one machine: left alone
# the router load-balances across endpoints weighted by price, and the
# endpoints differ in speed, in quantisation and in how much the model thinks
# before it answers. `order` is a preference with fallback, which is what you
# want — `only` with allow_fallbacks:false turns a provider's bad minute into
# a failed lesson:
#
# AI_EXTRA_BODY={"provider":{"order":["together","baseten","fireworks"]}}
#
# Pinning buys consistency as well as speed: `{"provider":{"quantizations":["fp8"]}}`
# fixes which quantisation serves you; the reasoning budget below fixes the
# thinking.
#
# REASONING IS THE BIGGER LEVER on a thinking model: left alone it can spend
# more tokens thinking than answering, and the learner waits through all of it
# and pays for all of it. A capped budget keeps the same answer and returns the
# first word in a fraction of the time:
#
# AI_EXTRA_BODY={"provider":{"order":["together"]},"reasoning":{"effort":"low"}}
#
# Cap it, do not switch it off: some endpoints refuse `{"enabled":false}` with
# a 400, and the app's harder jobs — authoring a curriculum, verifying an
# answer key — are the ones thinking was bought for. If lessons start arriving
# thin, raise it before blaming the model.
#
# The app's own fields always win, so nothing here can re-point a request at
# another model, un-stream a streaming reply or change a grader's temperature —
# it can only ADD. Anything that is not a JSON object is ignored with a warning.
# Leave it unset for a local engine, which understands none of it.
#
# Docker note: the app defaults to the HOST's Ollama via host.docker.internal.
# If you start the bundled one with `--profile with-ollama`, you must set
# AI_OLLAMA_URL=http://ollama:11434 or it keeps pointing at the host.


# --- Optional: Self-hosted SearXNG (Meta Search Engine) ----------------------
# If you run your own SearXNG instance (e.g. via Docker), you can configure
# its URL in the Settings UI (under AI Settings). This adds an extra layer of
# search results on top of the built-in DuckDuckGo / Wikipedia / GitHub sources.
#
# No environment variable needed — just set the URL in Settings.
#
# Quick start with Docker:
#   docker run -d -p 8080:8080 --name searxng searxng/searxng
#   Then open Settings → AI Settings → SearXNG URL → http://localhost:8080


# --- Read by the tools, not the app ------------------------------------------
# `node tools/leaf-invariant.mjs --api` checks the running server's counts
# against an independent recount.
#
# API_URL=http://localhost:3001


# --- Version identity and the update check -----------------------------------
# All optional. The app reports its version from package.json and its commit
# from `.git`; these cover the cases where neither is available or neither is
# right.
#
# GIT_SHA / BUILD_TIME — set at image build time by
# .github/workflows/release.yml, because there is no `.git` inside a container
# and "0.68.0" alone does not distinguish a release build from a local build of
# the same tag plus three commits. A hand-built image that omits them reports no
# commit rather than a wrong one. Do NOT set these on a git checkout: GIT_SHA
# overrides the working tree and would pin the reported commit to a stale value.
#
# GIT_SHA=
# BUILD_TIME=
#
# UPDATE_REPO_URL — where "Check for updates" looks and where the in-app "Get
# the source code" link points. Change it on a FORK, or the update check offers
# your users the upstream project's releases and the AGPL source link sends them
# to code they are not running.
#
# UPDATE_REPO_URL=https://github.com/ValeraZSD/terramentor
#
# TERRAMENTOR_DOCKER — forces the "you are in a container" verdict, which decides
# which update command the app prints. Detected automatically from /.dockerenv;
# this is the escape hatch for a runtime that has no such marker (Podman in some
# configurations, an unprivileged container).
#
# TERRAMENTOR_DOCKER=1
#
# TERRAMENTOR_DESKTOP / PORT_FALLBACK — set by desktop/launcher.js, never by hand.
# The first mounts the desktop lifecycle (heartbeat, quit, data-folder panel;
# server/desktop.js) and makes the app report a "desktop" install; the second
# lets the server move to the next port when 3001 is taken, which a developer
# running two servers does NOT want (EADDRINUSE is the right answer there).
#
# TERRAMENTOR_DESKTOP=1
# PORT_FALLBACK=1
